  "affiliation_unit": null,
  "affiliation_detail": "U.S. Health Sector Cybersecurity Coordination Cente (2023) states: \"It is unknown which area of the Chinese government they are affiliated with.\" FireEye/Mandiant (via Google Cloud) states: \"We believe APT22 has a nexus to China.\" Symantec traced Suckfly activity to three IP addresses in Chengdu, China and noted Monday-through-Friday operational schedule.\n",
  "affiliation_confidence": "suspected",
  "entity_name": null,
  "last_seen": "2016",
  "first_seen": "2014",
  "first_described": "2016",
  "description": "APT22 (also known as Suckfly, Barista, and Group 46) is a cyber espionage group active since at least early 2014. It is believed to be associated to the Chinese government.\nThe group is known for its capability to systematically steal code-signing certificates from South Korean companies to digitally sign its malware and hacking tools.\nSymantec's 2016 investigation traced APT22's activity to IP addresses in Chengdu, China, and documented the theft of nine code-signing certificates from Seoul-based companies across the software, gaming, entertainment, and financial sectors. These stolen certificates were used to sign malware, allowing the group's tools to appear as legitimate software and bypass security controls. \nAPT22's Indian targets included government entities responsible for implementing network software across ministries, one of India's largest financial organizations, a major e-commerce company and its shipping vendor, and a top-5 IT firm. (Symatec, 2016)\nFireEye/Mandiant separately tracks APT22 as \"Barista\" and documents the group's use of a broader malware arsenal (PISCES, SOGU/PlugX, FLATNOTE, ANGRYBELL, BASELESS, SEAWOLF, LOGJAM) and its targeting of healthcare organizations, particularly biomedical and pharmaceutical firms, and cancer research facilities.\n",
  "attack_group_id": "G0039",
